/* ==============================================================
   03 — MovePose: naming a position in space
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   Cartesian targets, and how they differ from joint ones
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     You name WHERE it ends up, not HOW it gets there
   TIME      ~20 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   FROM JOINTS TO SPACE

   MoveJoints asked for four joint positions. That is fine for
   the robot and useless for everyone else — nobody knows a
   part's location as a pair of arm angles.

   MovePose asks for a POSE instead: a position in ordinary
   three-dimensional space, and which way the tool is turned.

       MovePose(x, y, z, γ)

     x, y, z    where the tool tip is, in mm
     γ          how far the tool is turned about the vertical,
                in degrees

   Read it as: "put the TRF at this pose, expressed in the WRF."

     TRF — Tool Reference Frame. The tool tip. By default it sits
           on the end of the shaft; you move it to the real tip
           of the real tool with SetTrf. See 02_intermediate/02.
     WRF — World Reference Frame. The origin everything is
           measured from. By default it is the robot's base; you
           move it to a fixture or a tray with SetWrf. See
           02_intermediate/01.

   Those two frames are what turn a robot into an application,
   and they are the reason this command is worth the extra
   concept. A pose stays correct when you change tools or move
   the robot on the bench — you redefine a frame, and every pose
   in every program follows.

   WHERE THE AXES ARE

   The base frame sits on the bottom of the robot's base, on the
   axis of joint 1. x points straight forward, out of the front
   of the base. y points to the robot's left. z points up, so
   z is the height above the bench.

   ONE ANGLE, NOT THREE

   On a six-axis arm, orientation takes three angles, because
   the tool can point in any direction. On a SCARA it cannot: the
   shaft is always vertical, so the tool always points straight
   down. The only orientation left to choose is how far it is
   TURNED about that vertical. One number, γ.

   Every frame on this robot follows the same rule — z up, one
   angle about it — and the robot will not accept anything else.
   That removes a whole class of mistakes before you can make
   them.

   You still will not usually type these numbers. Jog the robot
   to the position you want and let the MecaPortal write them for
   you: right click in the code editor, then "TO CURRENT
   POSITION", and it inserts the command with the current pose
   already filled in, using the current TRF and WRF.

   THE IMPORTANT SUBTLETY

   MovePose specifies the DESTINATION in Cartesian space. It does
   NOT specify the path. Internally the robot solves for the
   joint positions that produce your pose, then performs a
   joint-space move to them — exactly like MoveJoints.

   So the tool tip travels a smooth, repeatable curve, not a
   straight line. If you need the straight line, that is MoveLin,
   and it is the next file.

     MoveJoints  target as joints      path is a curve
     MovePose    target as a pose      path is a curve
     MoveLin     target as a pose      path is a straight line

   MovePose is the one you will use most: it takes coordinates a
   human can reason about, while keeping the speed and freedom of
   a joint-space move.

   ONE MORE THING

   A single pose can usually be reached in two arm shapes — the
   elbow bent one way ("righty") or the other ("lefty") — and
   with the shaft on any of several turns. The robot picks
   automatically. When you need to force the choice, that is
   SetConf, in 02_intermediate/04.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)

// --- Start from the zero position -----------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)

/* This pose is the zero position expressed in Cartesian terms —
   the same robot posture as the line above, described the other
   way: 225 mm straight out, 109 mm up, tool not turned. Running
   it should produce no visible movement, which is the point: two
   vocabularies, one position. */
MovePose(225, 0, 109, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Now move in space ----------------------------------------
// Only z changed: 50 mm lower. And only ONE joint moves to do
// it — joint 3, the shaft. This is the defining property of a
// SCARA: up and down is a joint of its own, completely separate
// from where the arm is in the horizontal plane. On a six-axis
// arm, "lower the tool by 50 mm" takes several joints working
// together.
MovePose(225, 0, 59, 0)

Delay(1)

// Now across, in the horizontal plane. Joints 1 and 2 swing the
// arm — and watch the shaft: joint 4 turns too, even though γ
// did not change. It is holding the tool's direction fixed in
// the world while the arm around it rotates. Ask for γ = 0 and
// you get γ = 0, whatever the arm had to do.
MovePose(170, 60, 59, 0)

Delay(1)

// Watch the tool tip on this one. We ask for a move straight
// across and up, and the tip visibly bows out on the way. That
// curve is the joint-space path. Nothing is wrong — we simply
// never asked for a straight line.
MovePose(170, -60, 109, 0)

Delay(1)

// Only γ changed: the tool turns 90 degrees in place. One joint
// again — joint 4 — and the rest of the arm does not move.
MovePose(170, -60, 109, 90)

Delay(1)


// --- Back to zero ---------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Jog the robot anywhere by hand, then right click in the
      editor and insert MovePose "TO CURRENT POSITION". Teaching
      a point takes about four seconds. This lands well with
      anyone who has taught points on a pendant.

   2. Run the "across and up" MovePose and trace the curve with
      your finger. Then open 04_move_lin.mxprog and run the
      equivalent line. The difference between the two is the
      whole lesson.

   3. Try MovePose(240, 0, 100, 0). The robot refuses before
      moving: the arm is 225 mm long, and that point is 240 mm
      out. "Out of reach" is the first error everyone meets with
      Cartesian targets.

   NEXT   04_move_lin.mxprog — when the path itself matters.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
